PART 6 — CANDIDATE FILTERING AND RANKING LOGIC

This is the most important analytical logic.

I want option ranking to be personalized and explainable.

6A. Candidate filter using the participant’s strongest sensitivities

Use the participant’s top 3 sensitivities from the ranked threshold tree as the “gatekeeper” sensitivities.

Let:

top1
top2
top3

For each option, compute whether it passes threshold-like gates.

Gate rules

For each of the top 3 profile sensitivities:

the option “passes” that sensitivity if:
optionScore >= Math.max(30, userScore - 25)

Then determine candidate status:

An option is a candidate if:

it passes the participant’s top sensitivity
it passes at least 2 of the top 3 sensitivities

Store gate diagnostics so they can be shown in the explanation.

6B. Weighted fit score

For every option, compute a weighted fit score.

For each sensitivity dimension:

match = 100 - Math.abs(userScore - optionScore)

Then compute:

weightedFitScore = sum(match * dimensionWeight)

Use the user profile rank-derived weights from Part 1C.

Then apply:

+5 bonus if the option passes all top 3 sensitivity gates
-8 penalty if it fails the participant’s top sensitivity gate

Clamp final fit score to 0–100.

6C. Ranking

Sort options in this order:

all candidate options, sorted by fit score descending
all non-candidate options, sorted by fit score descending

Then assign display ranks:

1, 2, 3, 4, 5

Show clearly:

which option is the top-ranked candidate
which options are still candidates
which options are lower-fit / non-candidates
